# 5. PHASE TWO — GUIDED 3D PRESENTATION STUDIO

**Priority: HIGHEST PRODUCT DEVELOPMENT PRIORITY**

This is a central feature of the next generation of Anatomy 360.

The existing anatomy viewer already supports numerous individual visualization operations.

We now need the ability to combine these operations into a guided educational presentation.

Build a dedicated **Guided 3D Presentation Studio**.

It should allow students and teachers to demonstrate complicated anatomical processes without manually controlling every interaction.

## 5.1 Presentation architecture

Build a reusable presentation engine that uses the existing Three.js scene and interaction engine.

Do not create a second human anatomy renderer.

Each presentation should contain an ordered sequence of scenes.

Create a strongly typed data architecture similar to:

```ts
interface AnatomyPresentation {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  subject: string;
  learningObjectives: string[];
  level: string;
  sourceReferences: SourceReference[];
  steps: PresentationStep[];
}

interface PresentationStep {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  explanation: string;
  narration?: string;
  durationSeconds: number;

  camera: CameraConfiguration;

  scene: AnatomicalSceneConfiguration;

  annotations: AnatomicalAnnotation[];

  transition: TransitionConfiguration;
}
```

This is a starting schema, not a requirement to copy it without inspecting the current state architecture.

Support the following scene configuration features:

- Camera position and orientation.
- Focused anatomical region.
- Selected structures.
- Visible anatomical systems.
- Hidden structures.
- Isolated structures.
- Ghost transparency.
- Individual structure opacity.
- Anatomical highlighting.
- Color overrides.
- Full-body explosion.
- Group explosion.
- Progressive dissection.
- Cross-sectional clipping.
- Registered anatomical animations.
- Educational annotations.
- Captions and descriptions.

A presentation step should be able to reproduce a complete saved visualization deterministically.

